<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Task 1 Safe Passage, ADVANCED and DISRUPTIVE tier.

  Alongside task1_safe_passage.xml, not replacing it: Core tier still has no
  aircraft, and the runner picks the file by parameter so both stay loadable.

  WHAT IS DIFFERENT FROM CORE, and why.

  1. THE AIRCRAFT OWNS THE COLOURS. THE BOAT OWNS THE PLAN. handbook 3.3.2 says
     that above Core the passage is only detectable from the air, so ENTRY, EXIT
     and every beacon state come from the aircraft's ten-buoy field. But which
     pair is a gate, and what order to drive them in, is worked out HERE, by
     PlanPassage over the FUSED field (nav::planPassage over nav::fusePassage).
     The aircraft used to assign one pair at a time; it no longer does.

     Fusion is what makes the boat's own eyes worth having: the colour always
     comes from the aircraft, the POSITION comes from the tracker whenever a
     contact associates, because LiDAR range close in is far better than a
     photogrammetric fix from 60 m up, and metres at a gate are what matter.

  2. THE BOAT ENDS THE LOOP. It holds all ten buoys, so it can count its own
     gates: PassageComplete is "every gate in my plan is in cleared_gates". Core
     used `Inverter(NearExit radius=8)`, which is a guess and a wrong one - the
     boat must clear EVERY gate before the exit, and the exit buoy can sit well
     inside 8 m of the last one.

  3. THE ENTRY ORBIT AND EVERY GATE END IN A CONFIRMATION. Having crossed a pair
     (or finished circling the entry), the boat asks the
     aircraft whether the rest of the field has moved, and waits. The aircraft
     answers by acking the sequence number and retransmitting all ten buoys,
     which arrives as a new plan, so PlanPassage re-runs against it on the very
     next tick. That is the whole re-tasking mechanism: there is no separate
     path for "the passage changed".

     The sequence number counts CHECKPOINTS, not gates: seq 1 is the entry
     orbit, seq 2 is gate 1. Nothing downstream ties seq to a gate index, which
     is why adding the entry ask cost one XML line - cleared_gates is keyed on
     buoy ids.

     AwaitConfirmation re-asks every retry_s and gives up at timeout_s. The
     ForceSuccess around it is what "gives up" means - the boat drives on with
     the last field it was given rather than parking on the course forever. The
     WARN it logs is how that stays visible.

  4. A GATE IS REMEMBERED BY ITS TWO BUOYS, not by a counter. A confirmation can
     recolour the field and change the order; "I have done two, start at index
     two" then skips a gate that was never driven. MarkGateCleared records
     (red_id, green_id) and SelectNextGate skips what is in that set.

  5. A FAILED LEG RETRIES THE SAME GATE. MarkGateCleared is the last step of the
     leg, so anything failing before it leaves the gate uncleared and
     SelectNextGate hands back the same pair. The ForceSuccess around the leg is
     what makes a failure a RESTART rather than the end of the transit.

  THE GUARD BAND is re-ticked every tick and halts everything below it in one
  tick. PassagePlanFresh is in it twice over: it is the handbook's "the UAV must
  report before the USV may transit" gate, AND the dead-link stop.

  TIMEOUT AND CANCEL live in bt_runner_node, not here.
  OBSTACLE AVOIDANCE LIVES HERE NOW, not in the autopilot. ArduRover's OA_TYPE
  is a black box that cannot be watched on the tree view or tested in a pool;
  AvoidObstacles makes a detour an ordinary waypoint. It is local and reactive,
  one blocker at a time, recomputed every tick, and is not a planner.
-->
<root BTCPP_format="4" main_tree_to_execute="SafePassageDisruptive">
  <BehaviorTree ID="SafePassageDisruptive">
    <ReactiveSequence name="guard_band">

      <IsAutonomous/>
      <PassagePlanFresh max_age_s="15.0"/>
      <ResolveBuoyStates/>
      <PublishSafePassageReport min_period_s="1.0"/>

      <Sequence name="passage">

        <SetTask token="TASK_SAFE_PASSAGE"/>

        <!-- The entry buoy is known from the field the moment it arrives, so
             this drives straight at it instead of loitering to spot it. -->
        <CircleBuoy anchor="entry" radius="6.0" points="5" direction="cw"/>

        <!-- CONFIRM BEFORE THE TRANSIT STARTS, not only after each gate.
             Circling the entry is the last thing the boat does on a field
             nobody has re-checked since the goal was accepted, and it is the
             point at which the operator has had time to look at the passage and
             decide it is right. Asking here means the first gate is driven
             against a field confirmed seconds ago rather than minutes.

             This is the same pair of leaves the gates use, so the sequence
             number simply starts one earlier: seq 1 is the entry, seq 2 is
             gate 1. Nothing downstream counts on seq matching a gate index -
             cleared_gates is keyed on buoy ids, deliberately. -->
        <RequestConfirmation what="entry orbit"/>
        <ForceSuccess>
          <AwaitConfirmation timeout_s="120.0" retry_s="3.0"/>
        </ForceSuccess>

        <ForceSuccess>
          <KeepRunningUntilFailure name="transit">
            <ReactiveSequence>

              <!-- Re-planned at 10 Hz against the fused field, so a
                   confirmation carrying new colours or new positions is
                   picked up on the next tick with no special case. -->
              <PlanPassage max_width="20.0" min_width="2.0"/>

              <!-- One gate. Anything that fails inside restarts the leg. -->
              <ForceSuccess>
                <ReactiveSequence name="leg">
                  <Inverter>
                    <PlanChanged/>
                  </Inverter>
                  <Sequence>
                    <SelectNextGate/>

                    <!-- The run up to the gate is where the black buoys are,
                         so it is the leg that gets avoidance. AvoidObstacles
                         reads the LiDAR clusters by way of the tracker, plus
                         every passage buoy that is not this gate's own pair.

                         THE ReactiveSequence IS LOAD-BEARING. A plain Sequence
                         ticks AvoidObstacles once and then leaves NavigateTo
                         RUNNING for the rest of the leg, so the detour would be
                         computed from the boat's position at the START of the
                         run-in and never revised. Reactive re-ticks both every
                         tick: the detour follows the boat, and NavigateTo
                         re-sends the setpoint when it has moved far enough. -->
                    <GateWaypoint point="approach" standoff="6.0" approach="8.0" out="{goal}"/>
                    <ReactiveSequence name="run_in">
                      <AvoidObstacles in="{goal}" out="{steer}" clearance="5.0" margin="2.0"/>
                      <NavigateTo target="port" goal="{steer}" tolerance="3.0"/>
                    </ReactiveSequence>

                    <!-- The crossing itself is driven STRAIGHT, deliberately.
                         It is about 14 m between two buoys the boat must pass
                         on given sides, and a detour computed there could take
                         it round the wrong side of one of them. Water inside a
                         gate is the one place on this course meant to be clear. -->
                    <GateWaypoint point="through" standoff="6.0" approach="8.0" out="{goal}"/>
                    <NavigateTo target="port" goal="{goal}" tolerance="2.5"/>
                    <MarkGateCleared/>

                    <!-- "Is the next pair still where you said it was?" The
                         ForceSuccess is the deliberate give-up: see note 3.

                         TWO MINUTES, NOT TWENTY SECONDS, and the reason is that
                         the guard band already covers the case this timeout
                         looks like it is for. A dead radio is caught by
                         PassagePlanFresh above, within max_age_s, which halts
                         the whole mission rather than letting the boat drive an
                         unconfirmed field. What is left for this timeout is the
                         aircraft that is ALIVE and retransmitting but not
                         answering, which is a fault in the aircraft and not
                         something to give up on in a hurry.

                         Ekko will answer in milliseconds. The long window is for
                         the operator standing in for it, who is recolouring
                         buoys on a map before confirming - 20 s was not enough
                         for that, and the boat drove on twice in SITL with the
                         answer arriving a quarter of a second too late. -->
                    <RequestConfirmation what="gate"/>
                    <ForceSuccess>
                      <AwaitConfirmation timeout_s="120.0" retry_s="3.0"/>
                    </ForceSuccess>
                  </Sequence>
                </ReactiveSequence>
              </ForceSuccess>

              <!-- FAILURE here ends the transit, and it is the only thing that
                   should. The boat counts its own gates.

                   IT IS AFTER THE LEG, AND THAT ORDER IS THE WHOLE POINT. A
                   ReactiveSequence re-ticks every child above the running one on
                   every tick, so with this ABOVE the leg it was re-evaluated
                   while AwaitConfirmation was waiting. MarkGateCleared on the
                   LAST gate makes it true, so one tick later the leg was halted
                   and the transit ended - the boat asked the aircraft to confirm
                   and left 100 ms later without waiting for the answer.

                   Below the leg it is reached only BETWEEN legs, when the leg
                   has actually finished, confirmation included. SITL 2026-09-15:
                   a one-gate field asked once and went straight to RESULT, while
                   gate 1 of a two-gate field re-asked at 3, 6, 9, 12, 15 and 18 s
                   and timed out properly. Every gate except the last waited. -->
              <Inverter>
                <PassageComplete/>
              </Inverter>

            </ReactiveSequence>
          </KeepRunningUntilFailure>
        </ForceSuccess>

        <!-- From the aircraft's field, not from perception. Fails only if the
             aircraft never sent an exit at all. -->
        <ExitResolved/>
        <CircleBuoy anchor="exit" radius="6.0" points="5" direction="ccw"/>

      </Sequence>
    </ReactiveSequence>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
